  "FEATURE: `--artist` accepts a name, an ISNI, an IPI or a MusicBrainz UUID by shape. A code or ID is verified against MusicBrainz BEFORE the library walk; exactly one candidate carrying the code in its own `ipis`/`isnis` resolves, none or several is refused as `ArtistUnresolved`. A code is rejected with `--album`, in a batch line, and when `discography.enabled: false`. An identity works under the resolved canonical name (its work item, Soulseek query, placement and summary rows), while a name keeps its own spelling. Discography and failure-cache keys are prefixed `isni:`/`ipi:`/`mbid:`; `discography.artist_mbids` may be keyed by those strings, with printed separators tolerated, and the duplicate-key check uses the same normaliser. Near misses (a digit run one either side of 11 or 16, five hyphenated hex groups of 30..=34 digits) and the prefixed `isni:`/`ipi:`/`mbid:` form are configuration errors, rejected before any request. Only name resolution failures are recorded; an explicit target always bypasses the failure cache. The ISNI check character is `(12 - remainder) % 11`.\n\n" +
  "Four rounds of fixes have landed; this is the last configured round. Spend your effort on: (1) any path where a raw code or a code-shaped name reaches a Soulseek search or becomes a library folder name; (2) the cache/failure-cache key round trip, including an ISNI ending in `X` and a pinned code; (3) whether any guard is now unreachable, contradictory, or rejects something it should not (an ordinary name, a colon-bearing name, a padded UUID); (4) false comments/docs/README/spec claims; (5) one mutation of production code that the whole test suite would still pass.";

const results = await runs.all([
  { key: "reviewer-a", agent: "reviewer", task: task, model: "opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash:max" },
  { key: "reviewer-b", agent: "reviewer", task: task, model: "opencode-go/minimax-m3:max" },
]);
return results;

